# relmerge: the ffn rms_norm and the card leg q8_1 quantize folded into the 128-expert router launch of Inkling 2 Mini decode (934 -> 838 graph nodes, dN = -96), on main bd052ed29.
# Paper (specs/relmerge/census.md, re-read on bd052ed29 in specs/relmerge2/paper.md: #1219 (verify router gemv), #1220 (Bolt add3) and #1222 (pass side streams) touch no launch of the
# decode step this fold edits, body.rs is unchanged since e99204ffd): the step is one captured stream whose wall is the sum of its launches (host 0.15 ms after the replay, PCIe 8 B,
# host DRAM and NVMe 0, card DRAM 36 % busy = ~2 ms against a 5.43 ms step), so a removed launch comes off in full. Removed per visit, from the 7,793-replay nsys table of the 1,198-node
# graph (ingap2/plain1198.txt, medians): rms_norm 2.33 us + q3k_quantize_q8_1 1.04 us + two launch gaps 2 x 0.19 us = 3.75 us [measured]. Added per visit: the router blocks (32 blocks,
# each already reads the whole row) recompute the norm in front of their row walk (x and gain loads 0.7 us from L2, the warp butterfly and the eight-warp tree 0.2 us, the apply into shared
# memory 0.1 us) and warp 4 of 18 blocks writes the q8_1 planes beside the dot warps (+0.1 us) = 1.1 us [derived; the one term the derivation cannot fix, band 0.6..1.6]. Net 2.65 us x 48
# visits = 127 us [derived] (band 103..151). Step 1/184.03 = 5.434 ms (the inkland sitting, D=512 n=256 draft off) -> 5.307 ms = 188.4 tok/s, +2.4 % [derived]; band +1.9 .. +2.9 % (the
# added 0.6 .. 1.6 us). The decode step has no #1219/#1220/#1222 launch, so the base's 184.0 tok/s is the same step on bd052ed29; the base arm measures it again in the window.
kind: ab
question: does folding the ffn rms_norm and the card leg q8_1 quantize into the router launch (the router blocks recompute the norm; warps 4 to 7 quantize) remove its 127 us a token on the main base, i.e. is the router added latency about 1.1 us a visit and not over 2 us?
term: decode tok/s (tg, N - 1 decode steps) @ n=256, depth 512 (the first 512 ids of the model prose corpus), Inkling-2-Mini UD-Q4_K_XL whole on the RTX 5090, BLOOMERY_DRAFT=off, bloomery-serve one fresh server an arm and a round, arm new (main bd052ed29 + route_norm) over arm base (main bd052ed29), (new - base) / base, paired by round
unit: %
predict: 1.9..2.9
rounds: 6
sd: 0.6 % AGENTS.md Know the ruler (same-binary runs scatter with SD 0.6 %; the 6-round half-interval is 0.77 %)
condition: one hold, one runner call, BLOOMERY_MODEL=inkling2 BLOOMERY_AB_ROUNDS=6 just depth-serve with three arms, base first (arm k of round r is arm (k + r - 1) mod 3), each env=BLOOMERY_DRAFT=off (the seat serving default is the DSpark draft, which times the verify path the fold does not touch), DEPTH_SERVE_N=256, D 512, ctx unset (the plan picks it, the same in every arm). Arms: base (a clean build of origin/main bd052ed29), new (this tree), base2 (a byte copy of base: the A/A), each its own remote dir ~/repo/bloomery-relmerge2-{base,new,base2} holding its own binary (the sha256 of all three recorded before and after, the run builds nothing). Inside the window: each arm log carries `draft rule ... BLOOMERY_DRAFT=off`; the 5090 alone (no compute process but ours, ComfyUI not running, /root/agents/LEAD-TIMING-HOLD mine so the other rounds gates and builds wait); a row marked [cold] or [cpu-busy] is out of the means; the A/A pair (base2 over base) lands within 1 % of 0. The arms are two builds, so a difference under 1 % would carry the link layout; the band low edge is 1.9 %.
decide-in: the effect is inside the band: the fold is as derived, the table is the lease number for the PR (with the binaries sha256), the next fold candidates (census.md ranks 2 to 5, each under 1.5 %) stay in the ledger
decide-below: under +1.9 %: between +1.5 and +1.9 the router added latency is 1.6 to 2 us, report the effect and the saved us against 127; under +1.5 % the kill condition holds (the added latency eats the two removed launches: the norm in front of the row walk is on the critical path of the block), do not land, report the decomposition and the term that was wrong (the added latency), then an nsys round of the 838-node graph (needs a lease) for the router own time; a slow new arm (one round far from the others) first reruns the pair
decide-above: over +2.9 %: the removed kernels cost more in this graph than their 1,198-node medians, or the base arm is slow; read the per-round table and the A/A first, then rerun before crediting
